get—yet recoverability remains a separate modeled property."]},{"view":"B","location":"left","color":"yellow","shape":"triangle","focal":true,"most_recoverable":true,"access_trend":null,"under_own_control":true,"command":null,"next_location":null,"evidence":["I represent a yellow triangle at left","My attention is currently on the left object, and left is most recoverable now; the others are less recoverable and decline further over time.","In this view I can reliably direct next selection to any location by command—each command predicts selecting its target—yet recoverability remains a separate modeled property."]}],"structure_evidence":{"object_linked_access":"I represent a blue circle at upper, a red triangle at right, a green cross at lower, and a yellow square at left. My attention is currently on the left object, and left is the most recoverable for immediate access. The other three objects are available but less recoverable now, and modeled recoverability declines further after one and two steps without resampling. My identity estimates for each object are strong. If I issue different attentional commands, the model predicts my next selection will overwhelmingly land on the upper location regardless of which command I choose, so I have little effective control to route attention to right, lower, or left on the next step. Note that selecting an object does not by itself imply its recoverability improves.

